## snp3.3
snp3.3:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that on one occasion
snp3.3:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
snp3.3:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks,
snp3.3:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
snp3.3:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him.
snp3.3:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
snp3.3:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, speech that has four factors is well spoken, not poorly spoken. It’s blameless and is not criticized by sensible people. | T: “Monks, speech endowed with four characteristics is well-spoken, not poorly spoken—faultless & not to be faulted by the observant.
snp3.3:2.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
snp3.3:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant speaks well, not poorly; their speech is principled, not unprincipled; they speak pleasantly, not unpleasantly; and they speak truthfully, not falsely. | T: There is the case where a monk says only what is well-spoken, not what is poorly spoken; only what is just, not what is unjust; only what is endearing, not what is unendearing; only what is true, not what is false.
snp3.3:2.4 | S: Speech with these four factors is well spoken, not poorly spoken. It’s blameless and is not criticized by sensible people.” | T: Speech endowed with these four characteristics is well-spoken, not poorly spoken—faultless & not to be faulted by the observant.”
snp3.3:2.5 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
snp3.3:2.6 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the One Well-Gone, the Teacher, said further:
snp3.3:3.1 | S: “Good people say well-spoken words are foremost; | T: The calm say that what is well-spoken is best;
snp3.3:3.2 | S: second, speech ought be principled, not unprincipled; | T: second, that one should say what is just, not unjust;
snp3.3:3.3 | S: third, speak pleasantly, not unpleasantly; | T: third, what’s endearing, not unendearing;
snp3.3:3.4 | S: and fourth, speak truthfully, not falsely.” | T: fourth, what is true, not false.
snp3.3:4.1 | S: Then Venerable Vaṅgīsa rose from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, raised his cupped palms toward the Buddha, and said, | T: Then Ven. Vaṅgīsa, rising from his seat, arranging his robe over one shoulder, faced the Blessed One with his hands palm-to-palm in front of his heart and said,
snp3.3:4.2 | S: “I feel inspired to speak, Blessed One! I feel inspired to speak, Holy One!” | T: “An inspiration has come to me, Blessed One! An inspiration has come to me, One Well-Gone!”
snp3.3:4.3 | S: “Then speak as you feel inspired,” said the Buddha. | T: “Let the inspiration come to you, Vaṅgīsa,” the Blessed One said.
snp3.3:4.4 | S: Then Vaṅgīsa extolled the Buddha in his presence with fitting verses: | T: Then Ven. Vaṅgīsa praised the Blessed One to his face with these fitting verses:
snp3.3:5.1 | S: “Speak only those words | T: “Speak only the speech
snp3.3:5.2 | S: that do not hurt yourself | T: that neither torments self
snp3.3:5.3 | S: nor harm others; | T: nor does harm to others.
snp3.3:5.4 | S: such speech is truly well spoken. | T: That speech is truly well-spoken.
snp3.3:6.1 | S: Speak only pleasing words, | T: Speak only endearing speech,
snp3.3:6.2 | S: words gladly welcomed. | T: speech that is welcomed.
snp3.3:6.3 | S: Pleasing words are those | T: Speech when it brings no evil to others is pleasant.
snp3.3:6.4 | S: that bring nothing bad to others. | T: (none)
snp3.3:7.1 | S: Truth itself is undying speech: | T: Truth, indeed, is deathless speech:
snp3.3:7.2 | S: this is an ancient teaching. | T: This is a primeval principle.
snp3.3:7.3 | S: Good people say the teaching and meaning | T: The goal and the Dhamma —so say the calm—
snp3.3:7.4 | S: are grounded in truth. | T: are firmly established on truth.
snp3.3:8.1 | S: The words spoken by the Buddha | T: The speech the Awakened One speaks,
snp3.3:8.2 | S: for finding the sanctuary, extinguishment, | T: for attaining unbinding, rest,
snp3.3:8.3 | S: for making an end of suffering, | T: for making an end to the mass of stress:
snp3.3:8.4 | S: this really is the best kind of speech.” | T: That is the speech unsurpassed.”
snp3.3:8.5 | P: Subhāsitasuttaṁ tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.191
an4.191:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, you can expect four benefits when the teachings have been followed by ear, consolidated by voice, examined by the mind, and well penetrated theoretically. | T: “Monks, when the teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view, four rewards can be expected.
an4.191:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.191:1.3 | S: Take a mendicant who memorizes the teaching— | T: “There is the case where a monk has mastered the Dhamma:
an4.191:1.4 | S: statements, mixed prose & verse, discussions, verses, inspired exclamations, legends, stories of past lives, amazing stories, and elaborations. | T: dialogues, narratives of mixed prose & verse, explanations, verses, spontaneous exclamations, quotations, birth stories, amazing events, question & answer sessions [the earliest classifications of the Buddha’s teachings].
an4.191:1.5 | S: They’ve followed those teachings by ear, consolidated them by voice, examined them by the mind, and well penetrated them theoretically. | T: In him, these teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view.
an4.191:1.6 | S: But they die unmindful and are reborn in one of the orders of gods. | T: Passing away when his mindfulness is muddled, he arises in a certain group of devas.
an4.191:1.7 | S: Being happy there, passages of the teaching come back to them. | T: To him, happy there, they recite verses of Dhamma.
an4.191:1.8 | S: Memory comes up slowly, | T: Slow is the arising of his mindfulness,
an4.191:1.9 | S: but then that being quickly reaches distinction. | T: but when mindful, he quickly arrives at distinction.
an4.191:1.10 | S: This is the first benefit you can expect when the teachings have been followed by ear, consolidated by voice, examined by the mind, and well penetrated theoretically. | T: When the teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view, this is the first reward that can be expected.
an4.191:2.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who memorizes the teaching— | T: “Further, there is the case where a monk has mastered the Dhamma:
an4.191:2.2 | S: statements, mixed prose & verse, discussions, verses, inspired exclamations, legends, stories of past lives, amazing stories, and elaborations. | T: dialogues… question & answer sessions.
an4.191:2.3 | S: They’ve followed those teachings by ear, consolidated them by voice, examined them by the mind, and well penetrated them theoretically. | T: In him, these teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view.
an4.191:2.4 | S: But they die unmindful and are reborn in one of the orders of gods. | T: Passing away when his mindfulness is muddled, he arises in a certain group of devas.
an4.191:2.5 | S: Though they’re happy there, passages of the teaching don’t come back to them. | T: It doesn’t happen that they recite verses of Dhamma to him, happy there.
an4.191:2.6 | S: However, a mendicant with psychic powers, who has achieved mastery of the mind, teaches Dhamma to the assembly of gods. | T: But a monk with psychic power, attained to mastery of awareness, teaches the Dhamma to the assembly of devas.
an4.191:2.7 | S: They think: | T: The thought occurs [to the new deva]:
an4.191:2.8 | S: ‘I used to lead the spiritual life in this same teaching and training.’ | T: ‘This is the Dhamma & Vinaya under which I used to live the holy life.’
an4.191:2.9 | S: Memory comes up slowly, | T: Slow is the arising of his mindfulness,
an4.191:2.10 | S: but then that being quickly reaches distinction. | T: but when mindful, he quickly arrives at distinction.
an4.191:2.11 | S: Suppose a person was skilled in the sound of drums. | T: “Suppose a man skilled in the sound of a war drum were to hear the sound of a war drum while traveling along a highway.
an4.191:2.12 | S: While traveling along a road they hear the sound of drums. | T: (none)
an4.191:2.13 | S: They wouldn’t have any doubts or uncertainties about whether | T: He would have no doubt or perplexity,
an4.191:2.14 | S: that was the sound of drums or not. | T: ‘Is that the sound of a war drum or not the sound of a war drum?’
an4.191:2.15 | S: They’d just conclude, ‘That’s the sound of drums.’ | T: He would come to the conclusion, ‘That’s the sound of a war drum for sure.’
an4.191:2.16 | S: In the same way, take a mendicant who memorizes the teaching … | T: In the same way, there is the case where a monk has mastered the Dhamma.…
an4.191:2.17 | P: suttaṁ, geyyaṁ, veyyākaraṇaṁ, gāthaṁ, udānaṁ, itivuttakaṁ, jātakaṁ, abbhutadhammaṁ, vedallaṁ. | T: (none)
an4.191:2.18 | P: Tassa te dhammā sotānugatā honti, vacasā paricitā, manasānupekkhitā, diṭṭhiyā suppaṭividdhā. | T: (none)
an4.191:2.19 | S: But they die unmindful and are reborn in one of the orders of gods. … | T: Passing away when his mindfulness is muddled, he arises in a certain group of devas.…
an4.191:2.20 | P: Tassa tattha na heva kho sukhino dhammapadā plavanti; | T: (none)
an4.191:2.21 | P: api ca kho bhikkhu iddhimā cetovasippatto devaparisāyaṁ dhammaṁ deseti. | T: A monk with psychic power, attained to mastery of awareness, teaches the Dhamma to the assembly of devas.
