an4.191:2.26 | S: This is the second benefit you can expect when the teachings have been followed by ear, consolidated by voice, examined by the mind, and well penetrated theoretically. | T: When the teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view, this is the second reward that can be expected.
an4.191:3.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who memorizes the teaching— | T: “Further, there is the case where a monk has mastered the Dhamma:
an4.191:3.2 | S: statements, mixed prose & verse, discussions, verses, inspired exclamations, legends, stories of past lives, amazing stories, and elaborations. | T: dialogues… question & answer sessions.
an4.191:3.3 | S: They’ve followed those teachings by ear, consolidated them by voice, examined them by the mind, and well penetrated them theoretically. | T: In him, these teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view.
an4.191:3.4 | S: But they die unmindful and are reborn in one of the orders of gods. | T: Passing away when his mindfulness is muddled, he arises in a certain group of devas.
an4.191:3.5 | S: But passages of the teaching don’t come back to them when they’re happy, nor does a mendicant with psychic powers … teach Dhamma to the assembly of gods. | T: It doesn’t happen that they recite verses of Dhamma to him, happy there. Nor does a monk with psychic power, attained to mastery of awareness, teach the Dhamma to the assembly of devas.
an4.191:3.6 | S: However, a godling teaches Dhamma to the assembly of gods. | T: But a deva teaches the Dhamma to the assembly of devas.
an4.191:3.7 | S: They think: | T: The thought occurs [to the new deva]:
an4.191:3.8 | S: ‘I used to lead the spiritual life in this same teaching and training.’ | T: ‘This is the Dhamma & Vinaya under which I used to live the holy life.’
an4.191:3.9 | S: Memory comes up slowly, | T: Slow is the arising of his mindfulness,
an4.191:3.10 | S: but then that being quickly reaches distinction. | T: but when mindful, he quickly arrives at distinction.
an4.191:3.11 | S: Suppose a person was skilled in the sound of horns. | T: “Suppose a man skilled in the sound of a conch were to hear the sound of a conch while traveling along a highway.
an4.191:3.12 | S: While traveling along a road they hear the sound of horns. | T: (none)
an4.191:3.13 | S: They wouldn’t have any doubt about whether | T: He would have no doubt or perplexity,
an4.191:3.14 | S: that was the sound of horns or not. | T: ‘Is that the sound of a conch or not the sound of a conch?’
an4.191:3.15 | S: They’d just conclude, ‘That’s the sound of horns.’ | T: He would come to the conclusion, ‘That’s the sound of a conch for sure.’
an4.191:3.16 | S: In the same way, take a mendicant who memorizes the teaching … | T: In the same way, there is the case where a monk has mastered the Dhamma.…
an4.191:3.17 | P: suttaṁ, geyyaṁ, veyyākaraṇaṁ, gāthaṁ, udānaṁ, itivuttakaṁ, jātakaṁ, abbhutadhammaṁ, vedallaṁ. | T: (none)
an4.191:3.18 | P: Tassa te dhammā sotānugatā honti, vacasā paricitā, manasānupekkhitā, diṭṭhiyā suppaṭividdhā. | T: (none)
an4.191:3.19 | S: But they die unmindful and are reborn in one of the orders of gods. … | T: Passing away when his mindfulness is muddled, he arises in a certain group of devas.…
an4.191:3.20 | P: Tassa tattha na heva kho sukhino dhammapadā plavanti, napi bhikkhu iddhimā cetovasippatto devaparisāyaṁ dhammaṁ deseti; | T: (none)
an4.191:3.21 | P: api ca kho devaputto devaparisāyaṁ dhammaṁ deseti. | T: A deva teaches the Dhamma to the assembly of devas.
an4.191:3.22 | P: Tassa evaṁ hoti: | T: The thought occurs [to the new deva]:
an4.191:3.23 | P: ‘ayaṁ vā so dhammavinayo, yatthāhaṁ pubbe brahmacariyaṁ acarin’ti. | T: ‘This is the Dhamma & Vinaya under which I used to live the holy life.’
an4.191:3.24 | S: Memory comes up slowly, | T: Slow is the arising of his mindfulness,
an4.191:3.25 | S: but then that being quickly reaches distinction. | T: but when mindful, he quickly arrives at distinction.
an4.191:3.26 | S: This is the third benefit you can expect when the teachings have been followed by ear, consolidated by voice, examined by the mind, and well penetrated theoretically. | T: When the teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view, this is the third reward that can be expected.
an4.191:4.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who memorizes the teaching— | T: “Further, there is the case where a monk has mastered the Dhamma:
an4.191:4.2 | S: statements, mixed prose & verse, discussions, verses, inspired exclamations, legends, stories of past lives, amazing stories, and elaborations. | T: dialogues… question & answer sessions.
an4.191:4.3 | S: They’ve followed those teachings by ear, consolidated them by voice, examined them by the mind, and well penetrated them theoretically. | T: In him, these teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view.
an4.191:4.4 | S: But they die unmindful and are reborn in one of the orders of gods. | T: Passing away when his mindfulness is muddled, he arises in a certain group of devas.
an4.191:4.5 | S: But passages of the teaching don’t come back to them when they’re happy, and neither a mendicant with psychic powers … nor a godling teaches Dhamma to the assembly of gods. | T: It doesn’t happen that they recite verses of Dhamma to him, happy there. Nor does a monk with psychic power, attained to mastery of awareness, teach the Dhamma to the assembly of devas. Nor does a deva teach the Dhamma to the assembly of devas.
an4.191:4.6 | S: But a being who has been reborn spontaneously reminds another such being: | T: But [another] spontaneously-arisen being reminds [this] spontaneously-arisen being,
an4.191:4.7 | S: ‘Do you remember, good fellow? Do you remember where we used to lead the spiritual life?’ | T: ‘Do you remember, my dear? Do you remember where we practiced the holy life together?’
an4.191:4.8 | S: He says: | T: He says,
an4.191:4.9 | S: ‘I remember, good fellow, I remember!’ | T: ‘I remember, my dear. I remember.’
an4.191:4.10 | S: Memory comes up slowly, | T: Slow is the arising of his mindfulness,
an4.191:4.11 | S: but then that being quickly reaches distinction. | T: but when mindful, he quickly arrives at distinction.
an4.191:4.12 | S: Suppose there were two friends who had played together in the sand. | T: “Suppose that there were two comrades who played together in the mud.
an4.191:4.13 | S: Some time or other they’d meet. | T: They would happen to meet later at some time, at some place,
an4.191:4.14 | S: And one friend would say to the other: | T: and there one companion would say to the other,
an4.191:4.15 | S: ‘Do you remember this, my dear? Do you remember that, my dear?’ | T: ‘Do you remember this, my friend? And do you remember this?’
an4.191:4.16 | S: They’d say: | T: And the other would say,
an4.191:4.17 | S: ‘I remember, my dear, I remember!’ | T: ‘I remember, my friend. I remember.’
an4.191:4.18 | S: In the same way, take a mendicant who memorizes the teaching … | T: In the same way, there is the case where a monk has mastered the Dhamma:
an4.191:4.19 | P: suttaṁ, geyyaṁ, veyyākaraṇaṁ, gāthaṁ, udānaṁ, itivuttakaṁ, jātakaṁ, abbhutadhammaṁ, vedallaṁ. | T: dialogues… question & answer sessions.
an4.191:4.20 | P: Tassa te dhammā sotānugatā honti, vacasā paricitā, manasānupekkhitā, diṭṭhiyā suppaṭividdhā. | T: In him, these teachings have been followed by ear, recited by speech, examined by mind, and well penetrated by view.
an4.191:4.21 | S: But they die unmindful and are reborn in one of the orders of gods. … | T: Passing away when his mindfulness is muddled, he arises in a certain group of devas.…
an4.191:4.22 | P: Tassa tattha na heva kho sukhino dhammapadā plavanti, napi bhikkhu iddhimā cetovasippatto devaparisāyaṁ dhammaṁ deseti, napi devaputto devaparisāyaṁ dhammaṁ deseti; | T: (none)
an4.191:4.23 | P: api ca kho opapātiko opapātikaṁ sāreti: | T: A spontaneously-arisen being reminds [this] spontaneously-arisen being,
an4.191:4.24 | P: ‘sarasi tvaṁ, mārisa, sarasi tvaṁ, mārisa, yattha mayaṁ pubbe brahmacariyaṁ acarimhā’ti. | T: ‘Do you remember, my dear? Do you remember where we practiced the holy life together?’
an4.191:4.25 | P: So evamāha: | T: He says,
an4.191:4.26 | P: ‘sarāmi, mārisa, sarāmi, mārisā’ti. | T: ‘I remember, my dear. I remember.’
an4.191:4.27 | S: Memory comes up slowly, | T: Slow is the arising of his mindfulness,

