## an10.95
an10.95:1.1 | S: Then the wanderer Uttiya went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Uttiya the wanderer went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an10.95:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha, | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
an10.95:1.3 | S: “Worthy Gotama, is this right: ‘The cosmos is eternal. This is the only truth, anything else is futile’?” | T: “Master Gotama, is it the case that ‘The cosmos is eternal: Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless’?”
an10.95:1.4 | S: “This has not been declared by me, Uttiya.” | T: “Uttiya, I haven’t declared that
an10.95:1.5 | P: ‘sassato loko, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’”ti. | T: ‘The cosmos is eternal: Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless.’”
an10.95:2.1 | S: “Then is this right: ‘The cosmos is not eternal. This is the only truth, anything else is futile’?” | T: “Very well, then, Master Gotama, is it the case that: ‘The cosmos is not eternal: Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless’?”
an10.95:2.2 | S: “This has not been declared by me, Uttiya.” | T: “Uttiya, I haven’t declared that
an10.95:2.3 | P: ‘asassato loko, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’”ti. | T: ‘The cosmos is not eternal: Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless.’”
an10.95:3.1 | S: “Then is this right: ‘The cosmos is finite …’ … | T: “Very well, then, Master Gotama, is it the case that ‘The cosmos is finite … ’ …
an10.95:3.2 | S: ‘The cosmos is infinite …’ … | T: ‘The cosmos is infinite … ’ …
an10.95:3.3 | S: ‘The soul and the body are one and the same …’ … | T: ‘The soul & the body are the same … ’ …
an10.95:3.4 | S: ‘The soul is one thing, the body another …’ … | T: ‘The soul is one thing and the body another … ’ …
an10.95:3.5 | S: ‘A realized one still exists after death …’ … | T: ‘After death a Tathāgata exists … ’ …
an10.95:3.6 | S: ‘A realized one no longer exists after death …’ … | T: ‘After death a Tathāgata does not exist … ’ …
an10.95:3.7 | S: ‘A realized one both still exists and no longer exists after death …’ … | T: ‘After death a Tathāgata both does & does not exist … ’ …
an10.95:3.8 | S: ‘A realized one neither still exists nor no longer exists after death. This is the only truth, anything else is futile’?” | T: ‘After death a Tathāgata neither does nor does not exist. Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless’?”
an10.95:3.9 | S: “This has not been declared by me, Uttiya.” | T: “Uttiya, I haven’t declared that
an10.95:3.10 | P: ‘neva hoti na na hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇā, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’”ti. | T: ‘After death a Tathāgata neither does nor does not exist: Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless.’”
an10.95:4.1 | S: “When asked about all these points, worthy Gotama says that they have not been declared by him. | T: “But, Master Gotama, on being asked, ‘Is it the case that “The cosmos is eternal: Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless”?’ you inform me, ‘Uttiya, I haven’t declared that “The cosmos is eternal: Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless.”’ On being asked, ‘Is it the case that “The cosmos is not eternal … ” … “The cosmos is finite … ” … “The cosmos is infinite … ” … “The soul & the body are the same … ” … “The soul is one thing and the body another … ” … “After death a Tathāgata exists … ” … “After death a Tathāgata does not exist … ” … “After death a Tathāgata both does & does not exist … ” … “After death a Tathāgata neither does nor does not exist. Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless”?’ you inform me, ‘Uttiya, I haven’t declared that “After death a Tathāgata neither does nor does not exist. Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless.”’
an10.95:4.2 | P: sassato loko, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’ti vadesi. | T: (none)
an10.95:5.1 | P: ‘Kiṁ pana, bho gotama, asassato loko, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno: | T: (none)
an10.95:5.2 | P: ‘etampi kho, uttiya, abyākataṁ mayā asassato loko, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’ti vadesi. | T: (none)
an10.95:6.1 | P: ‘Kiṁ nu kho, bho gotama, antavā loko …pe… | T: (none)
an10.95:6.2 | P: anantavā loko … | T: (none)
an10.95:6.3 | P: taṁ jīvaṁ taṁ sarīraṁ … | T: (none)
an10.95:6.4 | P: aññaṁ jīvaṁ aññaṁ sarīraṁ … | T: (none)
an10.95:6.5 | P: hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇā … | T: (none)
an10.95:6.6 | P: na hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇā … | T: (none)
an10.95:6.7 | P: hoti ca na ca hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇā … | T: (none)
an10.95:6.8 | P: neva hoti na na hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇā, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno: | T: (none)
an10.95:6.9 | P: ‘etampi kho, uttiya, abyākataṁ mayā— | T: (none)
an10.95:6.10 | P: neva hoti na na hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇā, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’ti vadesi. | T: (none)
an10.95:6.11 | S: So what then has been declared by Worthy Gotama?” | T: Now is there anything you have declared?”
an10.95:7.1 | S: “Uttiya, I teach my disciples from my own insight in order to purify sentient beings, to get past sorrow and crying, to make an end of pain and sadness, to end the cycle of suffering, and to realize extinguishment.” | T: “Uttiya, having directly known it, I teach the Dhamma to my disciples for the purification of beings, for the overcoming of sorrow & lamentation, for the disappearance of pain & distress, for the attainment of the right method, & for the realization of unbinding.”
an10.95:8.1 | S: “But when the worthy Gotama teaches in this way, is the whole world saved, or half, or a third?” | T: “And, Master Gotama, when having directly known it, you teach the Dhamma to your disciples for the purification of beings, for the overcoming of sorrow & lamentation, for the disappearance of pain & distress, for the attainment of the right method, & for the realization of unbinding, will all the cosmos be led (to release), or a half of it, or a third?”
an10.95:8.2 | S: But when he said this, the Buddha kept silent. | T: When this was said, the Blessed One was silent.
an10.95:9.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to Ven. Ānanda:
an10.95:9.2 | S: “The wanderer Uttiya must not get the harmful misconception: | T: “Don’t let Uttiya the wanderer acquire the evil viewpoint that,
an10.95:9.3 | S: ‘When the ascetic Gotama was asked this all-important question he falters without answering. He just can’t do it!’ | T: ‘When I asked him an all-encompassing question, Gotama the contemplative faltered and didn’t reply. Perhaps he was unable to.’
an10.95:9.4 | S: That would be for his lasting harm and suffering.” | T: That would be for his long-term harm & suffering.”
an10.95:10.1 | S: Then Ānanda said to the wanderer Uttiya, | T: So he said to Uttiya,
an10.95:10.2 | S: “Well then, Reverend Uttiya, I shall give you a simile. | T: “In that case, my friend, I will give you an analogy,
an10.95:10.3 | S: For by means of a simile some sensible people understand the meaning of what is said. | T: for there are cases where it is through the use of analogy that intelligent people can understand the meaning of what is being said.
an10.95:10.4 | S: Suppose there was a king’s frontier citadel with fortified embankments, ramparts, and arches, and a single gate. | T: “Suppose that there were a royal frontier city with strong ramparts, strong walls & arches, and a single gate.
an10.95:10.5 | S: And it has a gatekeeper who is astute, competent, and intelligent. He keeps strangers out and lets known people in. | T: In it would be a wise, competent, & intelligent gatekeeper to keep out those he didn’t know and to let in those he did.
an10.95:10.6 | S: As he walks around the patrol path, he doesn’t see a hole or cleft in the wall, not even one big enough for a cat to slip out. | T: Walking along the path encircling the city, he wouldn’t see a crack or an opening in the walls big enough for even a cat to slip through.
an10.95:10.7 | S: He doesn’t know how many creatures enter or leave the citadel. | T: Although he wouldn’t know that ‘So-and-so many creatures enter or leave the city,’
an10.95:10.8 | S: But he does know that whatever sizable creatures enter or leave the citadel, all of them do so via this gate. | T: he would know this: ‘Whatever large creatures enter or leave the city all enter or leave it through this gate.’
an10.95:11.1 | S: In the same way, it’s not the Realized One’s concern whether the whole world is saved by this, or half, or a third. | T: “In the same way, the Tathāgata does not endeavor to have all the cosmos or half of it or a third of it led (to release) by means of (his Dhamma).
an10.95:11.2 | S: But the Realized One knows that whoever is saved from the world—whether in the past, the future, or the present—all have given up the five hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom. They have firmly established their mind in the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. And they have truly developed the seven awakening factors. | T: But he does know this: ‘All those who have been led, are being led, or will be led (to release) from the cosmos have done so, are doing so, or will do so after having abandoned the five hindrances—those defilements of awareness that weaken discernment—having well-established [‘well-tuned’] their minds in the four establishings of mindfulness, and having developed, as they have come to be, the seven factors for awakening.
an10.95:11.3 | S: That’s how they’re saved from the world, in the past, future, or present. | T: (none)
an10.95:11.4 | S: Uttiya, you were just asking the Buddha the same question as before in a different way. | T: When you asked the Blessed One this question, you had already asked it in another way.
an10.95:11.5 | S: That’s why he didn’t answer.” | T: That’s why he didn’t respond.”
an10.95:11.6 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an7.6
an7.6:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these seven kinds of wealth. | T: “Monks, there are these seven treasures.
an7.6:1.2 | S: What seven? | T: Which seven?
an7.6:1.3 | S: The wealth of faith, ethics, conscience, prudence, learning, generosity, and wisdom. | T: The treasure of conviction, the treasure of virtue, the treasure of a sense of shame, the treasure of a sense of compunction, the treasure of listening, the treasure of generosity, the treasure of discernment.
an7.6:2.1 | S: And what is the wealth of faith? | T: “And what is the treasure of conviction?
an7.6:2.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple has faith in the Realized One’s awakening … | T: There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones has conviction, is convinced of the Tathāgata’s awakening:
an7.6:2.3 | P: ‘itipi so bhagavā arahaṁ sammāsambuddho …pe… buddho bhagavā’ti. | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
an7.6:2.4 | S: This is called the wealth of faith. | T: This is called the treasure of conviction.
an7.6:3.1 | S: And what is the wealth of ethical conduct? | T: “And what is the treasure of virtue?
an7.6:3.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, commit sexual misconduct, use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical, or consume intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. | T: There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones abstains from taking life, abstains from stealing, abstains from sexual misconduct, abstains from lying, abstains from taking intoxicants that cause heedlessness.
an7.6:3.3 | S: This is called the wealth of ethical conduct. | T: This, monks, is called the treasure of virtue.
an7.6:4.1 | S: And what is the wealth of conscience? | T: “And what is the treasure of a sense of shame?
an7.6:4.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple has a conscience. They’re conscientious about bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind, and conscientious about having any bad, unskillful qualities. | T: There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones feels shame at (the thought of engaging in) bodily misconduct, verbal misconduct, mental misconduct. He feels shame at falling into evil, unskillful actions.
an7.6:4.3 | S: This is called the wealth of conscience. | T: This is called the treasure of a sense of shame.
an7.6:5.1 | S: And what is the wealth of prudence? | T: “And what is the treasure of a sense of compunction?
an7.6:5.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple is prudent. They’re prudent when it comes to bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind, and prudent when it comes to the acquiring of any bad, unskillful qualities. | T: There is the case where a monk, a disciple of the noble ones feels compunction at (the suffering that would result from) bodily misconduct, verbal misconduct, mental misconduct. He feels compunction at falling into evil, unskillful actions.
an7.6:5.3 | S: This is called the wealth of prudence. | T: This is called the treasure of a sense of compunction.
an7.6:6.1 | S: And what is the wealth of learning? | T: “And what is the treasure of listening?
an7.6:6.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple is very learned, remembering and keeping what they’ve learned. These teachings are good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased, describing a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. They are very learned in such teachings, remembering them, rehearsing them, mentally scrutinizing them, and penetrating them theoretically. | T: There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones has heard much, has retained what he/she has heard, has stored what he/she has heard. Whatever teachings are admirable in the beginning, admirable in the middle, admirable in the end, that—in their meaning & expression—proclaim the holy life that is entirely perfect, surpassingly pure: Those he/she has listened to often, retained, discussed, accumulated, examined with his/her mind, and well-penetrated in terms of his/her views.
an7.6:6.3 | S: This is called the wealth of learning. | T: This is called the treasure of listening.
an7.6:7.1 | S: And what is the wealth of generosity? | T: “And what is the treasure of generosity?
an7.6:7.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple lives at home with heart rid of the stain of stinginess, freely generous, open-handed, loving to let go, committed to charity, loving to give and to share. | T: There is the case of a disciple of the noble ones, his awareness cleansed of the stain of stinginess, living at home, is freely generous, openhanded, delighting in being magnanimous, responsive to requests, delighting in the distribution of alms.
an7.6:7.3 | S: This is called the wealth of generosity. | T: This is called the treasure of generosity.
an7.6:8.1 | S: And what is the wealth of wisdom? | T: “And what is the treasure of discernment?
an7.6:8.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple is wise. They have the wisdom of arising and passing away which is noble, penetrative, and leads to the complete ending of suffering. | T: There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones is discerning, endowed with discernment of arising & passing away—noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress.
an7.6:8.3 | S: This is called the wealth of wisdom. | T: This is called the treasure of discernment.
an7.6:9.1 | S: These are the seven kinds of wealth. | T: These, monks, are the seven treasures.”
an7.6:10.1 | S: Faith and ethical conduct are kinds of wealth, | T: The treasure of conviction, the treasure of virtue,
an7.6:10.2 | S: as are conscience and prudence, | T: the treasure of a sense of shame & compunction,
an7.6:10.3 | S: learning and generosity, | T: the treasure of listening, generosity,
an7.6:10.4 | S: and wisdom is the seventh kind of wealth. | T: & discernment as the seventh treasure.
