## sn42.8
sn42.8:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Nāḷandā in Pāvārika’s Mango Grove. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Nāḷandā in the Pāvārika Mango Grove.
sn42.8:1.2 | S: Then Asibandhaka’s son the chief, who was a disciple of the Jains, went up to the Buddha, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him, | T: Then Asibandhakaputta the headman, a disciple of the Nigaṇṭhas, went to the Blessed One and on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there the Blessed One said to him:
sn42.8:1.3 | S: “Chief, how does the Jain ascetic of the Ñātika clan teach his disciples?” | T: “Headman, how does Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta teach the Dhamma to his disciples?”
sn42.8:1.4 | S: “Sir, this is how the Jain Ñātika teaches his disciples: | T: “Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta teaches the Dhamma to his disciples in this way, lord:
sn42.8:1.5 | S: ‘Everyone who kills a living creature, steals, commits sexual misconduct, or lies goes to a place of loss, to hell. | T: ‘All those who take life are destined for a plane of deprivation, are destined for hell. All those who steal… All those who indulge in illicit sex… All those who tell lies are destined for a plane of deprivation, are destined for hell.
sn42.8:1.6 | S: You’re led on by what you frequently live by.’ | T: Whatever one keeps doing frequently, by that is one led (to a state of rebirth).’
sn42.8:1.7 | S: This is how the Jain Ñātika teaches his disciples.” | T: That’s how Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta teaches the Dhamma to his disciples.”
sn42.8:1.8 | S: “‘You’re led on by what you usually live by’: if this were true, then, according to what the Jain Ñātika says, no one would go to a place of loss, to hell. | T: “If it’s true that ‘Whatever one keeps doing frequently, by that is one led (to a state of rebirth),’ then no one is destined for a plane of deprivation or destined to hell in line with Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta’s words.
sn42.8:2.1 | S: What do you think, chief? | T: What do you think, headman?
sn42.8:2.2 | S: Take a person who kills living creatures. If we compare periods of time during the day and night, which is more frequent: the occasions when they’re killing or when they’re not killing?” | T: If a man is one who takes life, then taking into consideration time spent doing & not doing, whether by day or by night, which time is more: the time he spends taking life or the time he spends not taking life?”
sn42.8:2.3 | S: “The occasions when they’re killing are less frequent, while the occasions when they’re not killing are more frequent.” | T: “If a man is one who takes life, lord, then taking into consideration time spent doing & not doing, whether by day or by night, then the time he spends taking life is less, and the time he spends not taking life is certainly more.
sn42.8:2.4 | S: “‘You’re led on by what you usually live by’: if this were true, then, according to what the Jain ascetic of the Ñātika clan says, no one would go to a place of loss, to hell. | T: If it’s true that ‘Whatever one keeps doing frequently, by that is one led (to a state of rebirth),’ then no one is destined for a plane of deprivation or destined to hell in line with Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta’s words.”
sn42.8:3.1 | S: What do you think, chief? | T: “What do you think, headman?
sn42.8:3.2 | S: Take a person who steals … | T: If a man is one who steals…
sn42.8:3.3 | P: “Yo so, bhante, puriso adinnādāyī rattiyā vā divasassa vā samayāsamayaṁ upādāya appataro so samayo, yaṁ so adinnaṁ ādiyati, atha kho sveva bahutaro samayo, yaṁ so adinnaṁ nādiyatī”ti. | T: (none)
sn42.8:3.4 | P: “‘Yaṁbahulaṁ yaṁbahulañca, gāmaṇi, viharati tena tena nīyatī’ti, evaṁ sante na koci āpāyiko nerayiko bhavissati, yathā nigaṇṭhassa nāṭaputtassa vacanaṁ. | T: (none)
